1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,970
Here is a function that swaps two numbers.

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:07,267
Call it with ten and twenty, print them, and you get ten twenty.

3
00:00:07,267 --> 00:00:08,595
Nothing swapped.

4
00:00:08,595 --> 00:00:08,965
Why?

5
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:11,369
Because C passes copies.

6
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:15,236
The function swapped its own copies and threw them away.

7
00:00:15,236 --> 00:00:19,481
To reach the originals it needs their addresses: pointers.

8
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:22,793
By the end of this unit you will be able to fix it, and much more.

9
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:33,233
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 4: Programming in C.

10
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,669
Six parts.

11
00:00:36,669 --> 00:00:40,810
Functions: prototypes, definitions and calls.

12
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:42,229
Storage classes.

13
00:00:42,229 --> 00:00:43,258
Recursion.

14
00:00:43,258 --> 00:00:45,432
Structures and unions.

15
00:00:45,432 --> 00:00:49,286
Pointers, with call by value and call by reference.

16
00:00:49,286 --> 00:00:50,510
And data files.

17
00:00:54,867 --> 00:00:58,410
A function is a named block that does one job.

18
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:04,999
Library functions, like printf, come with C; user defined functions you write yourself.

19
00:01:04,999 --> 00:01:06,236
Three parts.

20
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:09,491
The prototype announces the function before main.

21
00:01:09,491 --> 00:01:15,767
The call, here inside printf, jumps to it with the arguments forty seven and ninety two.

22
00:01:15,767 --> 00:01:20,014
The definition is the body, and return sends the answer back.

23
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:21,797
It prints ninety two.

24
00:01:21,797 --> 00:01:27,498
Functions let you reuse code, test it piece by piece, and keep programs short and clear.

25
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:34,482
Call each counter three times.

26
00:01:34,482 --> 00:01:44,157
The automatic variable is created fresh, on the stack, every call: set to nought, increased to one, returned, destroyed.

27
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:45,446
One, one, one.

28
00:01:45,446 --> 00:01:51,748
The static variable is created once and lives for the whole program: one, two, three.

29
00:01:52,967 --> 00:02:00,547
A storage class decides where a variable lives, its starting value, where it can be seen, and how long it lasts.

30
00:02:00,547 --> 00:02:03,229
Auto is the default for local variables.

31
00:02:03,229 --> 00:02:07,318
Extern variables are global, visible everywhere.

32
00:02:07,318 --> 00:02:09,140
Static ones keep their value.

33
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,122
Register asks for a fast CPU register.

34
00:02:12,122 --> 00:02:18,295
And beware: an automatic variable you forget to initialise contains rubbish, not zero.

35
00:02:23,067 --> 00:02:26,050
Recursion is a function calling itself.

36
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:33,030
Every recursive function needs a base case that stops it, and a recursive case that moves towards it.

37
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:34,735
Call factorial of four.

38
00:02:34,735 --> 00:02:42,105
It needs factorial of three, which needs factorial of two, which needs factorial of one: the base case.

39
00:02:42,105 --> 00:02:43,879
Each waiting call sits on the stack.

40
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:46,003
Now the stack unwinds.

41
00:02:46,003 --> 00:02:48,789
Factorial of one returns one.

42
00:02:48,789 --> 00:02:50,560
Two times one is two.

43
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:52,747
Three times two is six.

44
00:02:52,747 --> 00:02:55,071
Four times six is twenty four.

45
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:58,164
Recursion against a loop.

46
00:02:58,164 --> 00:03:07,317
Recursion is shorter and mirrors the mathematical definition, but every call costs a stack frame, so it is slower and can overflow.

47
00:03:07,317 --> 00:03:13,051
The Fibonacci series is the other classic: each term is the sum of the two before it.

48
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:29,332
A structure is a user defined type that bundles several variables, of different types, under one name: a student's roll, name, faculty and marks.

49
00:03:29,332 --> 00:03:34,814
The definition is only a template; memory is used when you declare a variable.

50
00:03:34,814 --> 00:03:39,657
Every member gets its own space, so the size is the sum: forty bytes.

51
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:41,314
Reach a member with a dot.

52
00:03:41,314 --> 00:03:46,093
Strings must be copied with strcpy, not the equals sign.

53
00:03:46,093 --> 00:03:49,331
And an array of structures holds a whole class list.

54
00:03:50,533 --> 00:03:55,066
A union looks like a structure, but all its members share the same memory.

55
00:03:55,066 --> 00:03:59,389
Its size is the size of its largest member: eight bytes here.

56
00:03:59,389 --> 00:04:01,570
Only one member is valid at a time.

57
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,825
Store sixty five in i, and i reads sixty five.

58
00:04:05,825 --> 00:04:14,640
Store three point one four in f, and now i reads a huge meaningless number: the float's bits read as an integer.

59
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:17,466
Writing one member destroys the others.

60
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:21,286
Unions save memory when you need only one field at a time.

61
00:04:26,067 --> 00:04:29,194
Every variable lives at an address in memory.

62
00:04:29,194 --> 00:04:32,774
A pointer is a variable that stores such an address.

63
00:04:32,774 --> 00:04:38,490
Declare p as a pointer to an int, and set it to ampersand n, the address of n.

64
00:04:38,490 --> 00:04:39,628
Now p points at n.

65
00:04:39,833 --> 00:04:41,111
Two operators.

66
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:43,467
Ampersand gives an address.

67
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:50,577
Star, the indirection operator, follows the pointer to the value there: star p is twenty five.

68
00:04:50,577 --> 00:04:55,722
And star p equals star p plus five changes n itself, through the pointer.

69
00:04:56,933 --> 00:04:58,380
Back to the cold open.

70
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:01,557
In call by value, swap receives copies.

71
00:05:01,557 --> 00:05:05,696
It swaps the copies, and x and y in main never change.

72
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:11,787
In call by reference, swap receives the addresses, and works through them on the originals.

73
00:05:11,787 --> 00:05:14,079
Now x and y really are swapped.

74
00:05:14,079 --> 00:05:17,680
That is also how a function can return several results at once.

75
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,662
Pointers and arrays are close relatives.

76
00:05:21,662 --> 00:05:25,360
An array's name is the address of its first element.

77
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:33,229
Add two to the pointer and it moves two elements along, so star of q plus two is arr two: thirty.

78
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,314
Variables vanish when the program ends; a data file on disk survives.

79
00:05:43,314 --> 00:05:52,989
Every file program follows the same life: declare a file pointer, open the file with a mode, check it opened, read or write, and close it.

80
00:05:52,989 --> 00:06:00,557
The mode matters: w creates the file and erases anything already there, a appends to the end, r reads.

81
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:03,344
The file functions.

82
00:06:03,344 --> 00:06:14,112
Get c and put c handle one character; get w and put w one integer; f printf and f scanf work like printf and scanf, but with a file.

83
00:06:14,112 --> 00:06:18,123
F write and f read move whole structures.

84
00:06:18,123 --> 00:06:22,205
And f seek, f tell and rewind move around the file.

85
00:06:23,433 --> 00:06:28,799
Five integers written with put w, four bytes each: twenty bytes.

86
00:06:28,799 --> 00:06:32,549
Sequential access reads them in order from the start.

87
00:06:32,549 --> 00:06:40,817
Random access jumps: f seek to two times the size of an int, byte eight, lands on the third number, thirty three.

88
00:06:40,817 --> 00:06:45,218
After reading four bytes, f tell reports position twelve.

89
00:06:45,218 --> 00:06:49,490
The whole idea: offset equals record number times record size.

90
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:52,670
Pause and think.

91
00:06:59,967 --> 00:07:05,184
Call by reference: the function received an address and changed the original through it.

92
00:07:07,167 --> 00:07:07,602
One more.

93
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:19,115
Eight bytes: a union is as big as its largest member.

94
00:07:19,115 --> 00:07:21,460
A structure with the same members would add them up.

95
00:07:23,633 --> 00:07:24,715
Quick recap.

96
00:07:24,715 --> 00:07:28,491
Functions with prototypes, calls and definitions.

97
00:07:28,491 --> 00:07:29,950
Storage classes.

98
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:31,916
Recursion with its base case.

99
00:07:31,916 --> 00:07:34,025
Structures and unions.

100
00:07:34,025 --> 00:07:36,148
Pointers and call by reference.

101
00:07:36,148 --> 00:07:39,690
And data files, with random access by f seek.

102
00:07:43,867 --> 00:07:45,783
That was programming in C.

103
00:07:45,783 --> 00:07:52,540
Next, object-oriented programming: classes, objects, inheritance and polymorphism.

104
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:53,117
See you there.

